WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.040
How did a canine companion keep Steve-O sober?&nbsp;
Where did the part-time stuntman and full-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.240
troublemaker get his unmistakable voice? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.240
did he really go to clown college?&nbsp;
This is the untold truth of Steve-O.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.800
For Steve-O, the path to "Jackass"&nbsp;
stardom began long before the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.800
itself. He told Forbes that he first began&nbsp;
experimenting with videotaping when his dad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.800
won a camera from a golf tournament while&nbsp;
the comedian was still a teenager. Steve-O&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.040
took the camera from his dad's closet and used&nbsp;
it to film skateboard tricks with his friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.200
discovering that he had a real knack&nbsp;
and passion for filming in the process.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.720
It wasn't until after Steve-O went&nbsp;
to college at the University of Miami&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.640
that he got the idea to film more&nbsp;
elaborate stunts. He told Forbes,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.440
"I spent more time partying and jumping off the&nbsp;
diving boards at the pool than going to class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.840
which was why I got dumped by my girlfriend."

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.520
In the midst of heartbreak, Steve-O turned&nbsp;
back to filming and started to videotape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.360
himself performing dangerous stunts.&nbsp;
The reactions he got from people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.360
watched his tapes convinced him to drop&nbsp;
out of school and become a performer.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.560
Steve-O's stint at the University of&nbsp;
Miami wasn't the end of his education.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.840
He later attended the Ringling Bros. and Barnum&nbsp;
&amp; Bailey Clown College with 32 other classmates.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.160
Steve-O told Graham Bensinger that he got the&nbsp;
opportunity to work alongside another group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.400
of clowns on a cruise ship. The job only&nbsp;
lasted a season, though, because, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.320
"there was a clown mutiny." The other&nbsp;
clowns didn't get along with Steve-O,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.520
and they all threatened to leave if he returned&nbsp;
for another season, so the management fired him.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.680
Luckily, the job also gave Steve-O the&nbsp;
inspiration for a major stunt. Standing on stilts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.360
Steve-O would light his clown suit on fire, then&nbsp;
have a unicycler ride through his legs while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:47.040
a skateboarder jumped over his head and through&nbsp;
a fireball that he spit out of his mouth.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.760
For the finale, he'd chug a beer and tip over.&nbsp;
Steve-O called Jeff Tremaine to pitch him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.680
idea, and they filmed the stunt as a pilot for&nbsp;
MTV. The station refused to air the stunt but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.360
liked the idea and Steve-O&nbsp;
enough to create "Jackass."

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.360
Who knew that being an over-the-top stuntman&nbsp;
could land you in jail? Steve-O found out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.080
hard way in July 2002, when a performance at&nbsp;
a nightclub in Louisiana very nearly landed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.200
him behind bars with a felony. Two of the stunts&nbsp;
in Steve-O's performance came back to bite him.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.200
First, he received a principal to&nbsp;
second-degree battery charge for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.400
"presiding over" a stunt that ended in a club&nbsp;
bouncer knocking a college student unconscious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.200
Then he got a felony obscenity charge&nbsp;
for stapling his scrotum to his thigh.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.680
After much negotiation, First Assistant&nbsp;
District Attorney Mark Rhodes said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.480
the prosecution agreed that Steve-O hadn't&nbsp;
been an active participant in the battery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.600
and that his scrotum stapling was, quote,&nbsp;
"more than likely protected under free speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.160
when conducted in front of paying adult&nbsp;
patrons." Steve-O avoided the charges and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.960
a trial by agreeing to be on probation&nbsp;
for one year, donate $5,000 to a local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.280
shelter for battered women and children, and&nbsp;
to never perform in Terrebonne Parish again.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.080
The Louisiana incident wasn't the last time&nbsp;
one of Steve-O's stunts led him into legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.000
troubles. In fact, he was arrested again,&nbsp;
this time in Sweden, just a few months later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.760
He swallowed a condom full of marijuana and&nbsp;
snuck it through customs as a stunt for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.320
"Don't Try This At Home" show he and his fellow&nbsp;
"Jackass" members were performing in Stockholm.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.440
Steve-O's fans know him as a stuntman, and some&nbsp;
even know him as a stand-up comedian, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:32.160
many people are aware of Steve-O the rapper. Back&nbsp;
in 2011, he recorded and released the album "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.480
Dumbest Ass---e in Hip Hop" to not much fanfare at&nbsp;
all. The CD included a bonus DVD of more Steve-O&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.800
content and a poster of the album cover that read,&nbsp;
"Never before has a rapper been so ... dumb."

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.360
With tracks like "Crack Cocaine" and&nbsp;
"Poppin' Off," it's no surprise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.760
Steve-O's album didn't break the charts, but&nbsp;
that was likely never his goal to begin with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.720
Even Steve-O's fans more or less forgot the&nbsp;
album entirely the moment it was released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.160
Steve-O never released a follow-up album, and it&nbsp;
doesn't seem likely that he ever will, but fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.960
can always go back and enjoy one of the stranger&nbsp;
moments in the stuntman's career for themselves.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.440
Steve-O went through a massive lifestyle change&nbsp;
in the late 2000s. After years of performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.520
wild stunts partially fueled by drugs&nbsp;
and alcohol, he decided to get sober,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.880
and he's maintained that sobriety since 2008.&nbsp;
When talking about it, Steve-O makes what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.040
likely a lengthy and difficult decision&nbsp;
seem totally simple. He told Men's Health,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.320
"Here's what got me motivated to change: I&nbsp;
hit a wall. I really reached a point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.880
thank god, my life got so bad that I had&nbsp;
to surrender to the process of recovery."

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.840
The process has entirely transformed his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.200
and it's left him with plenty&nbsp;
of energy for more stunts.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.840
Of course, no one goes through recovery&nbsp;
alone, and Steve-O credits his rescue dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.560
Walter and Bernie, with keeping him going. He&nbsp;
adopted his first dog when he first embarked on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.320
the recovery journey in 2008, and he said it's&nbsp;
been good for him to, quote, "have a priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.920
that's not myself." Since then, Steve-O's dogs&nbsp;
have become his closest friends. They've helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.960
to deepen his connection with animals in general,&nbsp;
and that's also become a major part of his life.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.680
Steve-O has been an outspoken&nbsp;
animal rights activist for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.560
and sometimes he combines his passion for&nbsp;
ridiculous stunts with his love of animals to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.840
powerful results. In 2014, Steve-O and&nbsp;
five of his friends changed a "SeaWorld&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.520
Drive" sign just outside of San&nbsp;
Diego to say "SeaWorld Sucks."

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.920
"If doing that was wrong, I don't wanna be right."

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.720
That got people talking, but not as&nbsp;
much as Steve-O might have wanted, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.440
the following year, he upped the ante.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:45.360
Steve-O knows how to get attention, and climbing&nbsp;
a 100-foot crane certainly did the trick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.360
The machine hovered above Sunset&nbsp;
Boulevard, which gave the performer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:56.160
the perfect spot to speak to the world through&nbsp;
a giant Shamu balloon with just two words on it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.680
"SeaWorld Sucks." At the top of the crane,&nbsp;
Steve-O set off a series of fireworks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.000
making his way back to the ground, where he&nbsp;
was promptly arrested. From the streets below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.320
law enforcement couldn't tell whether the man on&nbsp;
the crane was suicidal, so they deployed a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:17.600
team to air on the side of caution — a helicopter,&nbsp;
five ambulances, and close to 100 firefighters.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.640
Prosecutors charged Steve-O with five misdemeanors&nbsp;
that could have landed him in jail for more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.280
four years. He reached a plea deal for 30 days&nbsp;
in jail, three years probation, and a $14,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.080
reimbursement to the city of Los Angeles. In&nbsp;
the end, Steve-O reported for his sentence at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.040
Twin Towers Correctional Facility, but he was&nbsp;
released after serving less than eight hours.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.000
In addition to fighting for animal rights with&nbsp;
attention-grabbing public displays, Steve-O&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.600
also altered his own lifestyle to align with his&nbsp;
values. He told Us Weekly that he became a vegan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.960
shortly after he started his recovery, and the&nbsp;
decision impacted way more than his diet. He said,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.600
"I think it's benefited every area of my life."

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.080
Since that interview, Steve-O hasn't&nbsp;
gone back to his meat-eating ways, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.200
these days he says that he's not a vegan. He's&nbsp;
technically a pescatarian because he'll eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.320
fish and eggs from time to time. That's not his&nbsp;
biggest slip-up, though. He told Men's Health,

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.600
"I've been letting a lot of dairy slide too.&nbsp;
Even admitting that, I have a problem with that."

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.680
Despite having been a vegan himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.920
Steve-O has also been critical of vegans&nbsp;
in the past. He wrote on Instagram,

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.840
"To all the combative, annoying vegans&nbsp;
who are choosing to attack me for not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.720
forcing veganism on my cats, and for&nbsp;
not being a strict, militant vegan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.360
myself […] maybe stop working so hard to pit&nbsp;
people who are on your team against you."

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.400
The post garnered plenty of backlash from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.400
vegan commenters, but Steve-O has&nbsp;
never been one to care about haters.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.760
Every "Jackass" fan is familiar&nbsp;
with Steve-O's voice. There's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.640
nothing quite like it. He has the gravelly&nbsp;
rumble of a cigarette-smoking blues singer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.600
but he's sounded like that for over 20 years.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.960
"I always thought that my world record for barfing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.160
the most on television might&nbsp;
have something to do with it."

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.400
Over the years, he's also speculated that his&nbsp;
unique tone could have come from swallowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.280
glass or consuming copious amounts of&nbsp;
nitrous oxide in his younger years.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:17.840
Eventually, Steve-O decided to consult with a&nbsp;
doctor to find out the root of his raspy voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:24.400
and the answer wasn't at all what he expected. It&nbsp;
turns out Steve-O sounds that way because he uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.080
his throat muscles rather than his vocal cords to&nbsp;
talk. To prevent his condition from getting worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.680
Steve-O started working on using&nbsp;
his vocal cords with a voice coach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.920
but he wasn't entirely sold on&nbsp;
changing his signature sound.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.000
"I don't even know if I want my voice to&nbsp;
change. I kind of like getting recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.080
over the phone — and my girl darn&nbsp;
sure doesn't want anything different."

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.520
"No, I love your voice."

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.280
Chances are, even if Steve-O&nbsp;
learns how to talk the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.400
"right way," he'll continue&nbsp;
to sound like he always has.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.280
After making his name as a stuntman,&nbsp;
Steve-O decided to stretch his abilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.480
and try out a new career path. He began&nbsp;
working as a stand-up comedian in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.960
and he's spent the past decade touring&nbsp;
various shows and approaching stand-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:12.880
with the same determination he once&nbsp;
aimed at his stuntman ambitions.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.240
Steve-O has completed two comedy specials:&nbsp;
"Gnarly," which was the first special he took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.600
on tour, and "The Bucket List," which he began&nbsp;
touring just as the coronavirus pandemic shuttered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.560
venues across the country. His stand-up acts have&nbsp;
always incorporated some of his stuntman roots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.320
but "The Bucket List" takes things to a whole new&nbsp;
level. Notably, he incorporates footage of brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.360
new stunts and stories into his act, creating&nbsp;
a multimedia experience for the audience.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.000
Despite all the work that Steve-O has put into&nbsp;
his stand-up acts, he's had a difficult time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:49.120
getting them out to anyone who isn't in his live&nbsp;
audience. He would gladly give the acts to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.560
streaming platform like Netflix, but so far, there&nbsp;
hasn't been any interest in his work. He spoke to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.160
Graham Bensinger in 2019 regarding the possibility&nbsp;
of his getting a comedy special on Netflix that,

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.960
"They only want to give them&nbsp;
to the most prestigious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:10.000
pure, you know, lifelong stand-up comedians,&nbsp;
of which they do not consider me to be one."

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.880
In the same interview with Graham Bensinger,&nbsp;
Steve-O explained that he and his fiancée&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.360
Lux Wright met on a job that "never ended&nbsp;
up seeing the light of day." Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.080
the project ended up being a failure, it gave&nbsp;
Steve-O the opportunity to meet the love of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.560
life. The two of them don't plan on ever having&nbsp;
kids, but they still have big plans for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.680
future — opening an animal sanctuary where they'll&nbsp;
eventually be able to live when they retire.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.360
"You don't have to know us very&nbsp;
well to know that we love animals."

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.520
Steve-O told Bensinger,

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.040
"We want to have it in Southern&nbsp;
California, so we can drive to it."

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.520
Ideally, it would also operate as a bed&nbsp;
and breakfast that would give people the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.840
opportunity to stay longer and develop deeper&nbsp;
relationships with the animals they're visiting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.560
Steve-O said he hopes it gives people pause the&nbsp;
next time they go to eat an animal for dinner.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:01.040
He went on to explain that in the long term,&nbsp;
the sanctuary would also become a daycare for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.600
children with special needs. Steve-O's&nbsp;
nephew has Down syndrome and autism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.400
and he has witnessed the stress his sister is&nbsp;
put under while trying to manage her career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.560
and take care of her children. He wants&nbsp;
to be able to give back to parents with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:19.360
similar struggles, and his fiancée is more&nbsp;
than happy to create that legacy with him.

